    <!-- ── Section 1: Overview ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="overview" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Overview</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">QA Review Studio is a quality engineering platform focused on <strong>Specification Driven Development (SDD)</strong> — connecting governance rules, requirements, implementation plans, and tasks in a single traceable, governable workflow that spans the full delivery lifecycle.</p>

        <div class="ug-cap-grid">
            @foreach (var cap in CoreCapabilities)
            {
                <div class="ug-cap-card">
                    <div class="ug-cap-icon" aria-hidden="true">@cap.Icon</div>
                    <div class="ug-cap-name">@cap.Name</div>
                    <div class="ug-cap-desc">@cap.Desc</div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Who uses it?</h3>
        <div class="ug-audience-grid">
            @foreach (var (Role, Desc) in Audiences)
            {
                <div class="ug-audience-card">
                    <div class="ug-audience-role">@Role</div>
                    <div class="ug-audience-desc">@Desc</div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 2: Core Concepts ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="concepts" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Core Concepts</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Understanding the four artifact types and the analysis categories that operate across them.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Artifacts</h3>
        <p>QA Review Studio governs quality across four distinct artifact types. Each artifact is a structured markdown document loaded into the platform for analysis.</p>

        <div class="ug-concept-grid">
            @foreach (var a in ArtifactConcepts)
            {
                <div class="ug-concept-card">
                    <div class="ug-concept-name">@a.Name</div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-file ug-fd-mono">@a.File</div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-desc">@a.Desc</div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-feature">Primary feature: <a href="@a.Route" class="ug-inline-link">@a.Feature</a></div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Analysis</h3>
        <p>Four analysis categories work across the artifact set. Each category answers a distinct governance question.</p>

        <div class="ug-concept-grid">
            @foreach (var a in AnalysisConcepts)
            {
                <div class="ug-concept-card">
                    <div class="ug-concept-name">@a.Name</div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-question"><em>@a.Question</em></div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-desc">@a.Desc</div>
                    <div class="ug-concept-feature">Feature: <a href="@a.Route" class="ug-inline-link">@a.Feature</a></div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">AI Review</h3>
        <p>AI Review features augment the deterministic analysis layer with AI-powered advisors. AI Review is disabled by default for new installations and development environments. Admins can enable it from <a href="/admin/system-settings" class="ug-inline-link">System Settings</a> → <strong>Feature Visibility</strong> by turning on <strong>AI Change Review</strong>.</p>
        <div class="ug-concept-grid">
            <div class="ug-concept-card">
                <div class="ug-concept-name">AI Change Review</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-question"><em>Are AI-generated changes aligned to the spec?</em></div>
                <div class="ug-concept-desc">AI-assisted review of implementation changes for alignment, quality, and risk. Use after AI generates or modifies implementation work.</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-feature">Feature: <a href="/ai-change-auditor" class="ug-inline-link">AI Change Review</a></div>
            </div>
            <div class="ug-concept-card">
                <div class="ug-concept-name">Quality Review</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-question"><em>Does the artifact set meet the quality bar?</em></div>
                <div class="ug-concept-desc">Run multiple deterministic review packs simultaneously — compliance, audit, standards, and readiness checks — in a single unified run. Select any combination of packs for each review session.</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-feature">Feature: <a href="/quality-review" class="ug-inline-link">Quality Review</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 3: Feature Matrix ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="features" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Full Feature Matrix</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Every feature at a glance — what it does, what it takes, what it produces, and when to reach for it.</p>

        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Section</th>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Input</th>
                        <th>Output</th>
                        <th>When To Use</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var f in AllFeatures)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td><span class="ug-area-badge ug-area-@f.AreaKey">@f.Area</span></td>
                            <td class="ug-td-feature"><a href="@f.Route" class="ug-inline-link">@f.Name</a></td>
                            <td>@f.Purpose</td>
                            <td class="ug-td-mono">@f.Input</td>
                            <td>@f.Output</td>
                            <td>@f.WhenToUse</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 4: SDD Workflow ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="workflow" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">SDD Workflow</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">The workflow is dynamic and workspace-aware. The <a href="/getting-started" class="ug-inline-link">Recommended Workflow</a> page is the reviewer/release workflow: it adapts based on loaded artifacts, explicit manual review, and release approval progress. Developer diagnostics are kept under System Settings -> Developer and do not count as workflow approvals.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">The Recommended Workflow Page</h3>
        <p>Visit <a href="/getting-started" class="ug-inline-link">/getting-started</a> for a live, interactive guide that:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Shows your workspace</strong> — project name, artifact count, and readiness badges for Constitution, Specification, Plan, Tasks, and Data Model</li>
            <li><strong>Recommends your next action</strong> — a "Next Recommended Action" card shows the most valuable step based on what's loaded and what you've completed</li>
            <li><strong>Highlights the current step</strong> — the workflow step you should focus on is visually emphasized</li>
            <li><strong>Locks unavailable steps</strong> — steps that require artifacts you haven't loaded yet are marked as "Locked" until their prerequisites are met</li>
            <li><strong>Shows manual approval status</strong> — artifacts can unlock pages automatically, but only reviewer actions mark steps as reviewed, pending approval, approved, or needing changes</li>
            <li><strong>Excludes developer diagnostics</strong> — Environment Diagnostics, ReviewContext Validation, Runtime Diagnostics, and Documentation Health stay under System Settings → Developer</li>
            <li><strong>Updates in real-time</strong> — the workflow refreshes automatically when you load artifacts, run analysis, or toggle features from Feature Visibility</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Typical Workflow Sequence</h4>
        <p>While the workflow is dynamic, a typical sequence looks like this:</p>
        <div class="ug-core-flow-layout">
            <div class="ug-core-flow">
                <div class="ug-flow-node ug-flow-secondary">1 · Load Artifacts</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-conn">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-node ug-flow-secondary">2-5 · Review Supporting Artifacts</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-conn">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-node ug-flow-primary">6 · Run Reviews & Analysis</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-conn">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-node ug-flow-primary">7 · Quality Review</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-conn">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-flow-node ug-flow-secondary">8 · Optional: AI Change Review or Advanced Analysis</div>
            </div>

            <div class="ug-flow-branches">
                <h4 class="ug-branches-title">Feature Visibility Control</h4>
                <div class="ug-branch-item">
                    <span class="ug-branch-badge">AI Review</span>
                    <span class="ug-branch-when">Disabled by default. Enable from System Settings → Feature Visibility → AI Review to add AI Change Review to the workflow.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-branch-item">
                    <span class="ug-branch-badge">Legacy Traceability</span>
                    <span class="ug-branch-when">Hidden by default. Enable from System Settings → Feature Visibility → Traceability Navigation if your team still uses legacy Code Traceability or Traceability Suggestions.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-branch-item">
                    <span class="ug-branch-badge">Developer Tools</span>
                    <span class="ug-branch-when">System Settings → Developer includes ReviewContext Validation and runtime diagnostics for developer/admin use only. These checks are not release approval steps.</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Key insight:</strong> The Recommended Workflow is not a diagnostics console. It is a release-review guide that evolves with your workspace. Start by loading artifacts, then follow the "Next Recommended Action" card. Use System Settings → Developer for internal consistency checks.
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 5: Workspace ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="workspace" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Workspace</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Your workspace is the collection of loaded artifacts and analysis results that drive the entire platform. Understanding workspace state is key to using QA Review Studio effectively.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Workspace Status Panel</h3>
        <p>Every page that works with artifacts displays a workspace status panel showing:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Current project name</strong> — which sample project or workspace is active</li>
            <li><strong>Artifact count</strong> — how many of the five supported artifacts are loaded (Constitution, Specification, Plan, Tasks, Data Model)</li>
            <li><strong>Artifact readiness badges</strong> — a badge for each artifact showing loaded (✓) or missing (○) status with color coding</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Workspace States</h3>
        <p>Your workspace can be in three states:</p>
        <div class="ug-concept-grid">
            <div class="ug-concept-card">
                <div class="ug-concept-name">Empty Workspace</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-desc">No artifacts are loaded. The Recommended Workflow shows an empty state and recommends loading a sample project. Most review pages are inaccessible.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ug-concept-card">
                <div class="ug-concept-name">Partial Workspace</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-desc">Some artifacts are loaded, but not all five. The Recommended Workflow adapts to what's available and locks steps that require missing artifacts. Quality Review gracefully skips missing artifact analysis.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ug-concept-card">
                <div class="ug-concept-name">Full Workspace</div>
                <div class="ug-concept-desc">All five artifacts are loaded. The Recommended Workflow shows all steps as available. Run Quality Review with any combination of packs for a complete analysis.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">Loading Artifacts</h3>
        <p>Load artifacts using <a href="/sample-projects" class="ug-inline-link">Sample Projects</a>. This page scans configured project folders, shows which artifacts are supported and which are missing, and loads them into your workspace with one click. Once loaded, artifacts remain in the workspace across all pages until you load a different project.</p>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Workspace persistence:</strong> Your loaded artifacts and analysis results persist across all pages and browser sessions until you load a different project. You do not need to re-upload files when switching between analysis features.
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 6: Typical Workflows ────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="workflows" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Typical Workflows</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Common delivery scenarios and the features that support each one.</p>

        <div class="ug-workflow-grid">
            @foreach (var wf in TypicalWorkflows)
            {
                <div class="ug-workflow-card">
                    <div class="ug-workflow-title">@wf.Title</div>
                    <div class="ug-workflow-flow">
                        @for (var i = 0; i < wf.Steps.Length; i++)
                        {
                            <span class="ug-wf-step">@wf.Steps[i]</span>
                            @if (i < wf.Steps.Length - 1)
                            {
                                <span class="ug-wf-arrow">→</span>
                            }
                        }
                    </div>
                    <div class="ug-workflow-desc">@wf.Desc</div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 6: Feature Details ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="feature-details" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Feature Details</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Expand any feature for full details — purpose, inputs, outputs, recommended users, and when to use or avoid it.</p>

        <div class="ug-feature-grid">
            @foreach (var f in AllFeatures)
            {
                var isOpen = _openFeature.Contains(f.Id);
                <div class="ug-feature-card ug-fcard-@f.AreaKey @(isOpen ? "is-open" : "")">
                    <div class="ug-feature-header" @onclick="() => ToggleFeature(f.Id)">
                        <div>
                            <span class="ug-area-badge ug-area-@f.AreaKey">@f.Area</span>
                            <div class="ug-feature-name">@f.Name</div>
                            <div class="ug-feature-purpose">@f.Purpose</div>
                        </div>
                        <button class="ug-expand-btn" aria-expanded="@isOpen.ToString().ToLower()">@(isOpen ? "▲" : "▼")</button>
                    </div>
                    @if (isOpen)
                    {
                        <div class="ug-feature-body">
                            <div class="ug-fd-row">
                                <div class="ug-fd-item">
                                    <div class="ug-fd-label">Input</div>
                                    <div class="ug-fd-value ug-fd-mono">@f.Input</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="ug-fd-item">
                                    <div class="ug-fd-label">Output</div>
                                    <div class="ug-fd-value">@f.Output</div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="ug-fd-item">
                                <div class="ug-fd-label">Best Used By</div>
                                <div class="ug-fd-roles">
                                    @foreach (var role in f.BestUsedBy)
                                    {
                                        <span class="ug-role-badge">@role</span>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="ug-fd-item">
                                <div class="ug-fd-label">Use When</div>
                                <ul class="ug-fd-list">
                                    @foreach (var when in f.UseWhen)
                                    {
                                        <li>@when</li>
                                    }
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <div class="ug-fd-item">
                                <div class="ug-fd-label">Avoid When</div>
                                <ul class="ug-fd-list ug-fd-list-avoid">
                                    @foreach (var avoid in f.AvoidWhen)
                                    {
                                        <li>@avoid</li>
                                    }
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <a href="@f.Route" class="ug-feature-link-btn">Open @f.Name →</a>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 7: Where Do I Find This? ────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="where" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Where Do I Find This?</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Quick navigation reference — every feature, its purpose, and where it lives in the sidebar.</p>

        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Menu Section</th>
                        <th>Route</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var nav in NavReference)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td class="ug-td-feature"><a href="@nav.Route" class="ug-inline-link">@nav.Name</a></td>
                            <td>@nav.Purpose</td>
                            <td><span class="ug-area-badge ug-area-@nav.SectionKey">@nav.Section</span></td>
                            <td class="ug-td-mono">@nav.Route</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Feature Visibility:</strong> Sidebar menu visibility is controlled from <a href="/admin/system-settings" class="ug-inline-link">System Settings</a> → <strong>Feature Visibility</strong>. Disabled modules keep their implementation, routes, and components intact, but they are hidden from the main menu until an admin enables them.
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 8: Helpful Scenarios ────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="scenarios" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Helpful Scenarios</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Common questions and the features that answer them.</p>

        <div class="ug-scenario-list">
            @foreach (var s in HelpfulScenarios)
            {
                <div class="ug-scenario-item">
                    <div class="ug-scenario-q">@s.Question</div>
                    <div class="ug-scenario-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="ug-scenario-a">
                        <a href="@s.Route" class="ug-inline-link">@s.Feature</a>
                        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(s.Note))
                        {
                            <span class="ug-scenario-note"> — @s.Note</span>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 9: Feature Discovery ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="discovery" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Feature Discovery</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Several powerful analysis views are embedded as tabs inside existing workflows — they do not have dedicated menu items. This section tells you exactly where to find each one and how to activate it.</p>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Key rule:</strong> Run analysis first, then explore the result tabs. Once analysis completes, switching between tabs does not require re-uploading files — the session is preserved automatically.
        </div>

        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>Found Inside</th>
                        <th>Route</th>
                        <th>How To Access</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Traceability &amp; Coverage</strong></td>
                        <td>Specification Review</td>
                        <td class="ug-td-mono">/extract</td>
                        <td>Analyse a spec → active by default in Analysis Results</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Flow View</strong></td>
                        <td>Specification Review</td>
                        <td class="ug-td-mono">/extract</td>
                        <td>Analyse a spec → click <strong>Flow View</strong> tab in Analysis Results</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Spec Explorer</strong></td>
                        <td>Specification Review</td>
                        <td class="ug-td-mono">/extract</td>
                        <td>Analyse a spec → click <strong>Spec Explorer</strong> tab in Analysis Results</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Risk &amp; Testing</strong></td>
                        <td>Implementation Review</td>
                        <td class="ug-td-mono">/task-alignment</td>
                        <td>Analyse spec.md + tasks.md → click <strong>Risk &amp; Testing</strong> tab in results</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-spec-explorer">Spec Explorer</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Spec Explorer is the specification structure and navigation layer. Use it to understand how the specification is organized, browse sections and headings, see which artifacts belong to each section, identify healthy versus unhealthy sections, and navigate into Traceability.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Spec Explorer</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Navigating a large or complex specification by structure rather than by extracted item</li>
            <li>Seeing section health, including requirements, tests, clarifications, coverage percentage, and explicit Covered, Partial, or Missing labels</li>
            <li>Understanding User Story ownership without expanding every section</li>
            <li>Opening a requirement or section and jumping into Traceability when coverage needs deeper analysis</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Role clarity:</strong> Traceability &amp; Coverage is for coverage analysis. Flow View is for QA readiness. Spec Explorer is for specification structure and navigation.
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-flow-view">Flow View</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> A QA Readiness dashboard for User Stories. Flow View groups requirements, acceptance tests, success criteria, and implementation tasks by user story — and surfaces gaps, readiness status, and QA Ready scores without requiring manual inspection of each story. It answers: <em>Which User Stories are ready for QA and release?</em></p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Flow View</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Story Readiness Status</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Ready</strong> — requirements, tests, success criteria, and implementation links are present.</li>
            <li><strong>At Risk</strong> — tests exist, but success criteria or implementation links are missing.</li>
            <li><strong>Blocked</strong> — requirements are missing linked acceptance tests.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-traceability-view">Traceability &amp; Coverage (Analysis Tab)</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Shows the QA risk picture first: coverage status, missing tests, orphan tests, missing success criteria, missing user story mappings, and relationship gaps. This is the <em>default</em> view shown after analysis.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>. The Traceability &amp; Coverage tab is active by default in Analysis Results.</p>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Traceability &amp; Coverage vs. Artifact Traceability:</strong> Traceability &amp; Coverage shows relationships <em>within the specification</em> (requirements to tests). <a href="/artifact-traceability" class="ug-inline-link">Artifact Traceability</a> shows relationships <em>across all four artifacts</em> (Constitution → Spec → Plan → Tasks).
        </div>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Traceability &amp; Coverage vs. QA Artifact Library:</strong> Coverage analysis belongs in Traceability &amp; Coverage. The QA Artifact Library is a repository for reusable QA assets that teams can discover and reuse after review; it is not the place where coverage health is calculated.
        </div>
        <p><strong>Navigation:</strong> Click a health summary card, such as Missing User Stories or Missing Success Criteria, to open the Gaps tab as a focused QA work queue.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Gap Categories</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Missing User Story:</strong> A coverage requirement is not mapped to a User Story.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Success Criteria:</strong> A coverage requirement has no linked measurable outcome.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Tests:</strong> A coverage requirement has no linked acceptance tests.</li>
            <li><strong>Orphan Tests:</strong> A test exists but is not linked to any coverage requirement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-task-explorer">Task Explorer</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Task Explorer is the <strong>Implementation Coverage</strong> workspace. It answers whether the specification is being implemented: which requirements have tasks, which requirements are missing implementation, and where delivery risk exists.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Task Explorer has its own menu item in the REVIEW section (<a href="/task-explorer" class="ug-inline-link">/task-explorer</a>). It is also available as an enriched tab inside Implementation Review after a full analysis.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 10: AI-Assisted Development ─────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="ai-dev" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">AI-Assisted Development</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">QA Review Studio supports teams using AI to accelerate implementation. AI Review is disabled by default; enable AI Change Review from System Settings → Feature Visibility when your team needs an AI-assisted post-generation review.</p>

        <div class="ug-ai-flow-row">
            <div class="ug-ai-flow">
                <div class="ug-ai-node">
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-label">Constitution</div>
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-desc">Governance rules, policies, quality standards</div>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-arrow">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-ai-node">
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-label">Specification</div>
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-desc">Requirements, user stories, acceptance criteria</div>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-arrow">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-ai-node">
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-label">Plan + Tasks</div>
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-desc">Architecture decisions, phases, implementation tasks</div>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-arrow">↓</div>
                <div class="ug-ai-node ug-ai-node-terminal">
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-label">Quality Review</div>
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-desc">Compliance, audit, and readiness gates in one run</div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="ug-ai-concepts">
                <div class="ug-ai-concept">
                    <div class="ug-ai-concept-title">Specification-First Development</div>
                    <p>Every task and implementation decision traces back to a requirement. QA Review Studio enforces this by surfacing alignment gaps early using Artifact Traceability and Implementation Review.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-concept">
                    <div class="ug-ai-concept-title">Governance</div>
                    <p>Constitution Explorer establishes the governance rule set. Quality Review's Constitution Compliance pack verifies that all artifacts comply with those rules before development begins.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-concept">
                    <div class="ug-ai-concept-title">Traceability</div>
                    <p>Artifact Traceability maintains a complete chain from governance rule to implementation task. Quality Review's QA Auditor pack identifies missing traceability links with deterministic rule-based findings.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="ug-ai-concept">
                    <div class="ug-ai-concept-title">Readiness</div>
                    <p>Quality Review's QA Readiness and Delivery Readiness packs aggregate all analysis outputs into scored readiness gates — clearing the team to proceed with confidence through Development, Testing, and Release.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Tip:</strong> When using AI to generate tasks from a spec, run Implementation Review immediately after to catch any tasks that introduce undocumented behavior. Then run Quality Review to surface rule-based findings across the full artifact set.
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 11: Validation ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="validation" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">Validation</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">Developer/admin validation tools verify that the runtime workspace and canonical ReviewContext metrics are coherent before relying on downstream analysis results.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title">ReviewContext Validation</h3>
        <p><strong>Where:</strong> <a href="/admin/system-settings" class="ug-inline-link">System Settings</a> → Developer → ReviewContext Validation. This developer-only section is visible in Development mode and is intended for admins and maintainers, not normal end-user workflows.</p>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> It validates the canonical ReviewContext created from loaded workspace artifacts and compares its metrics with downstream analysis sources. Use it when diagnosing inconsistent counts, missing artifacts, or suspected parser/traceability regressions.</p>

        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Status</th>
                        <th>Meaning</th>
                        <th>Typical action</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>PASS</strong></td>
                        <td>Canonical ReviewContext metrics and available downstream comparisons are consistent.</td>
                        <td>Proceed with review, analysis, and quality checks.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>WARNING</strong></td>
                        <td>Validation ran but some context is incomplete, unavailable, or only partially comparable.</td>
                        <td>Check which artifacts are loaded and whether partial-workspace behavior is expected.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FAIL</strong></td>
                        <td>A canonical metric or downstream comparison is inconsistent enough to block trust in the result.</td>
                        <td>Treat as a developer investigation item before relying on affected analysis output.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Workspace behavior</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Empty workspace:</strong> The section shows a helpful empty state and validation reports that no ReviewContext is available. Load supported artifacts first.</li>
            <li><strong>Partial workspace:</strong> Validation still runs against the artifacts that are loaded. Missing artifact metrics may appear as warnings or zero counts; this is expected for focused reviews.</li>
            <li><strong>Loaded workspace:</strong> The Loaded Artifacts table lists constitution.md, spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, and data-model.md status before validation results are reviewed.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Exports</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Export JSON:</strong> Copies the full validation report for debugging, automated comparison, or issue attachment.</li>
            <li><strong>Export HTML:</strong> Copies a readable report that can be shared with maintainers or included in a diagnostic note.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Screenshots:</strong> The User Guide currently does not embed screenshots for navigation, Feature Visibility, Sample Projects, review pages, analysis pages, or System Settings. No screenshot replacements are required in this page.
        </div>
    </section>

